course: dictionary
lesson: 4874
title: Ideal: perfect, or just a dream?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ideal is a word you will meet often. As an adjective it means perfect, or the best possible for something. «It was an ideal day for a picnic.» The sun was out and nothing was spoiled, so ideal means the best possible for a picnic.
explain: Ideal can also mean something that is not real yet. «In an ideal world, nobody would be hungry.» Nobody can say that is true today, so the sentence describes how things should be, like a dream. Look at the words around ideal to tell which meaning you need.
explain: Ideal can be a noun too, so it names a thing or a belief. «She is my ideal of a good teacher» means she is the perfect example of what a good teacher is. Ideals, with an s, are beliefs about how things should be, such as «the ideals of fairness and freedom».
explain: Use the swap check to test your meaning. Does «an ideal day for a picnic» still make sense if you say «a perfect day for a picnic»? Yes, so you have the right meaning.
fact: Ideal means perfect, or the best possible for something.
fact: «Ideal» can also mean existing only in the mind, not real, as in «an ideal world».
fact: As a noun, an ideal is a belief about how things should be, and «ideals» are beliefs such as fairness and freedom.
fact: «Not ideal» (mostly UK) means bad or not good enough.
fact: In science, «ideal conditions» are the best conditions for a plant or an experiment.
exercise: «The park was an ideal place for a picnic.» What does ideal mean here? | Far away from home | A place nobody has heard of | *The best possible place for a picnic | Very cold and wet
exercise: «In an ideal world, nobody would be hungry.» What does ideal mean here? | *Existing only in the mind, not real yet | Very noisy | Perfect for eating | Hungry all the time
exercise: «Ella's ideals are fairness and kindness.» What are ideals here? | *Beliefs about how things should be | A sharp tool | A kind of food | A type of football team
test: «She is my ideal of a good teacher.» What does ideal mean here? | A new classroom | A teacher who is always late | A school bell | *A perfect example of a good teacher
test: Lily says, «That plan is not ideal.» What does she mean? | It is excellent | It is the best plan ever | *It is not good enough, or it is bad | It is very funny
exam: Which sentence uses ideal as a noun meaning a belief? | «In an ideal world, nobody would be hungry.» | «The ideal conditions helped the plant grow.» | *«She holds high ideals of fairness and freedom.» | «It was an ideal day for a picnic.»
